-- title:
--   Scheja–Storch trace form for a square presentation
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative ring, let $m$ be a natural number and let $f \colon \mathrm{Fin}\,m \to R[x_1,\dots,x_m]$ be a family of $m$ polynomials in $m$ variables, written $f_i$. Assume that the quotient $A := R[x_1,\dots,x_m]/(f_1,\dots,f_m)$, the quotient by the ideal generated by the range of $f$, is finite and free as an $R$-module. Then there exists an $R$-linear form $\tau \colon A \to R$ with the following two properties. First, the map sending $a \in A$ to the composite of left multiplication by $a$ with $\tau$, i.e. the map $A \to \operatorname{Hom}_R(A,R)$, $a \mapsto \tau(a\,\cdot\,)$, is bijective; thus $\operatorname{Hom}_R(A,R)$ is free of rank one over $A$ on the generator $\tau$. Second, for every $a \in A$ the trace of the $R$-linear endomorphism of $A$ given by multiplication by $a$ satisfies $\operatorname{Tr}_{A/R}(a) = \tau(\bar J \cdot a)$, where $\bar J$ is the image in $A$ of the determinant of the $m \times m$ Jacobian matrix whose $(i,j)$ entry is the partial derivative $\partial f_i/\partial x_j$.
--
--   This is the Scheja–Storch description of the dualising module of a square complete intersection: such an $A$ is Gorenstein over $R$, and the trace form is the canonical generator twisted by the Jacobian determinant. It is the engine behind the comparison of the discriminant of $A/R$ with the norm of $\bar J$, and is cited by [`Algebra.associated_discr_norm_jacobianDet_of_square_presentation`](thm.html#Algebra.associated_discr_norm_jacobianDet_of_square_presentation).
